<!-- FIXTURE
proves: a GFM task item's continuation line is PROSE. Prettier indents it
past the checkbox, to column 6, while strict CommonMark puts the item's
content column at 2 — and 6 - 2 = 4, which is exactly the indented-code
threshold. Reading the spec column here deletes the continuation from the
scan and reports the file clean, so this is the fail-open regression that
`TASK_MARKER` in checks/split-code-spans.py exists to prevent. Both
checkbox states are covered because they are separate marker shapes.
expect: 16 "the the"
expect: 19 "in in"
-->

# Task-list continuation

- [ ] **Step 1:** a description long enough that prettier wraps it onto
      a second line, where the the doubling lives.

- [x] A checked item wraps its continuation the same way, and a pair can
      land in in the middle of it.
